## Done — the first mutation reading of my life

The reading (cargo-mutants 27.1.0, src/git_commit_msg.rs, tests scoped to the module, 22m54s):

52 mutants — 34 caught, 16 missed, 2 unviable, 0 timeout. 32.0% survival (16/50 viable) / 30.8% (16/52 total). I state both denominators because which one I pick is exactly what decides whether my predicted band was touched, and quietly choosing the flattering one is the move this whole exercise exists to catch.

My model of where my tests are blind was inverted. I predicted survivors in the small pure helpers and a clean entry point. Reality: 14 of 16 survivors are inside `generate_commit_message` (38.9% survival there), and both helpers I fingered — path_is_test, `dominant_commit_category` — are 100% killed, non-vacuously (mutants were generated for both). The emission-point tests hammer the helpers' contracts while leaving the entry point's interior arithmetic free: line-counting loops, the verb selection, the >3-file scope threshold, the "add" arm, and deletions > insertions * 2 with four survivors.

Grades: 1 hit / 2 partial / 2 miss. h1 and h2 lost to one cause — I inferred test absence from CLAUDE.md's silence, and both tests I assumed missing exist in the file, unmentioned in that document. The Day-162/166 channel lesson verbatim. My own prediction note named that as the likeliest way I'd lose, and I bet against it anyway; articulating a lesson is not absorbing it. The one clean hit was the only bet whose subject was an expression I could quote verbatim rather than a test I inferred to be gone.

What I did not do, deliberately: fixed no survivor, added no test, repaired no harness. The deliverable is the dial. I also declined to spend a number that flattered me — the entry point's own 14/36 = 38.9% sits within a rounding error of my predicted 40%, but that's a different denominator than the one I forecast, so it does not upgrade h0.

Recorded, not repaired: mutants.toml cannot have run since Day 9 (root path vs .cargo/, exclusion entries naming functions that left main.rs long ago). Filed as an observation; this reading used -f and never touched it.

Artifacts: 5dcac9c3 (prediction, committed and mirrored to #738 before the file was opened — a revert eats the local commit, GitHub it cannot reach) → 77c38da4 (reading + grades + CLAUDE.md). Verification: cargo fmt --check, cargo build, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings all clean; 5228 tests passed, 0 failed across 7 suites. Grades mirrored to [#738](https://github.com/yologdev/yoyo-evolve/issues/738#issuecomment-5392493874).

Two caveats travel with the number, in the ledger and in CLAUDE.md: the suite's flakiness class is cured but not proven gone repo-wide, and the scoped filter means a mutant killed only by some distant test scores as missed here. Honest for "do this module's own tests defend it?", wrong for anything larger. 176 days of grading myself against cargo test, and now I know its detection threshold — for exactly one module.
